---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import ResultsTable from '../../components/ResultsTable.astro';
import Fig from '../../components/Fig.astro';
import Tex from '../../components/Tex.astro';
import QrngSampler from '../../components/figures/QrngSampler.astro';
import setup from '../../assets/figures/fq-setup.png';
import nist from '../../assets/figures/fq-nist.png';
import encrypt from '../../assets/figures/fq-encrypt.png';
import bitrate from '../../assets/figures/fq-bitrate.png';

const src = 'Kolangatt et al., Phys. Rev. A 110, 032615 (2024). © 2024 American Physical Society.';
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="four-qubit-qrng"
  setting="Quantum Optics & Quantum Information, Department of Instrumentation and Applied Physics, IISc Bengaluru"
  contribution={[{ text: 'I worked on the theory, data collection, noise modelling and testing.', basis: 'Author’s statement, October 2026.' }]}
  collaborators={[
    {
      text: 'Mayalakshmi Kolangatt (first author), Sujai Matta, Kanad Sengupta and C. M. Chandrashekar. The paper does not state individual author roles.',
      basis: 'Author list, Phys. Rev. A 110, 032615.',
    },
    {
      text: 'The paper thanks K. Muhammed Shafi for input on the design of the experimental setup, and M. Joshi for contributing to building the entangled photon source.',
      basis: 'Acknowledgments, Phys. Rev. A 110, 032615.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <Fragment slot="problem">
    <p>
      Most random numbers in practice come from algorithms, which are predictable to anyone who knows the algorithm
      and its seed. Quantum measurements give genuine randomness, but statistical tests alone cannot show where a
      sequence came from, and testing a sequence normally means revealing it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This work asks how to let an outside party verify the randomness of a quantum device in real time while the bits
      actually used stay secret, and how to generate public and private keys from the same system.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="method">
    <p>The protocol uses a four-qubit state that is an equal superposition of all even-parity strings, Eq. (2) of the paper:</p>
    <Tex block tex={String.raw`|\psi\rangle_{ABCD}=\tfrac{1}{2\sqrt{2}}\bigl(|0000\rangle+|0101\rangle+|0110\rangle-|0011\rangle+|1100\rangle-|1001\rangle-|1010\rangle-|1111\rangle\bigr)`} />
    <p>Measuring each qubit gives four bit strings that are pairwise independent but tied by a parity rule, Eq. (5):</p>
    <Tex block tex={String.raw`x_A \oplus x_B \oplus x_C \oplus x_D = 0`} />
    <p>
      Discarding one string breaks that link, leaving three independent strings. Publishing one for randomness testing
      then certifies the other two without revealing them, so n published bits verify 2n secret bits.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the experiment, polarization-entangled photon pairs come from a type-II PPKTP crystal pumped at 405 nm. Beam
      splitters add a path qubit to each photon, giving a four-qubit polarization–path state read out by eight
      single-photon detectors and a time tagger. The same outputs give Alice and Bob simultaneous public and private
      keys, demonstrated by encrypting and decrypting images. A noise model, applied to the two-photon input state,
      describes how entanglement variation and depolarizing noise change the bit rate.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="results">
    <p>
      The system produced publicly verified and secured random bits at <Claim id="fq.verified-rate" />, from a
      collective rate of <Claim id="fq.collective-rate" bare /> at 6 mW pump power. Of the sampled bits,{' '}
      <Claim id="fq.verification" /> passed entanglement verification, and all strings passed{' '}
      <Claim id="fq.nist" bare /> NIST tests without post-processing.
    </p>
    <ResultsTable ids={['fq.verified-rate', 'fq.collective-rate', 'fq.verification', 'fq.nist', 'fq.source-fidelity', 'fq.visibility', 'fq.chsh', 'fq.noise-model']} />
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="interactive">
    <p>
      The sampler draws outcomes from the ideal state above and splits them as the protocol does: one string published,
      two kept secret, one discarded.
    </p>
    <QrngSampler />
    <p class="caption">
      For the ideal state the parity rule always holds. In the experiment, <Claim id="fq.verification" bare /> of the
      sampled bits satisfied it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="figures">
    <Fig src={setup} alt="Optical layout: 405 nm laser, PPKTP crystal, source characterisation insets, and Alice's and Bob's measurement units." caption="Fig. 3. Experimental setup, with the reconstructed density matrix and visibility curves of the source as insets." source={src} wide />
    <Fig src={nist} alt="Bar chart of p-values for 15 NIST tests for strings XA, XB and XC, all above the 0.01 threshold." caption="Fig. 4. NIST randomness test results for the strings XA, XB and XC." source={src} />
    <Fig src={encrypt} alt="Two images shown original, encrypted by Alice as noise, and decrypted by Bob." caption="Fig. 5. Encryption and decryption of images using the generated private and public keys." source={src} />
    <Fig src={bitrate} alt="Two plots of CHSH value and bit rate against visibility and against half-wave-plate angle, experiment and theory." caption="Fig. 7. Measured bit rate and CHSH value for different entanglement visibilities, with the theoretical expectation." source={src} />
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
